say when an operation announced it finished, and whether a completion block ran

0069 could say an operation STARTED and that start returned with it still running, which is normal
for a concurrent operation. It could not say whether the operation ever came back. That is the
difference between an indexer that is stuck and one that finished while the interface ignored it,
and iA Writer needed exactly that distinction (#194).

Two more lines, on the same gate. kvo prints every isReady, isExecuting and isFinished the
operation announces, which is how a concurrent subclass reports itself; completion prints whether a
completion block was set and dispatched.

WHAT IT SAID, and it KILLED my hypothesis rather than confirming it:

  CIDER_OP kvo IALibraryIndexingOperation isFinished
  CIDER_OP completion IALibraryIndexingOperation none set

Every IALibraryIndexingOperation finishes. Every IAContentBlockProcessingOperation finishes and its
completion block is dispatched. Nothing in the indexing pipeline is stuck, so the library rows that
say Updating... forever are not waiting on an operation.
